//Constraints & Conjunction explantion with its examples
 1. Constraint
##Definition
A constraint is a condition or rule that must be satisfied in a problem.
It limits the possible solutions.

## Example
Constraint:
x > 5
If x = 8 → ✔ Satisfies (8 > 5)
If x = 3 → ✖ Does NOT satisfy (3 > 5 is false)
## Real-Life Example
Constraint:
Age must be 18 or above to vote.
Age = 20 → ✔ Allowed
Age = 16 → ✖ Not allowed
 2. Conjunction (AND Operator ∧)
## Definition
A conjunction combines two statements using AND (∧).
It is true only when both statements are true.

## Logical Form
P ∧ Q
# Truth Conditions (Explained Clearly)
If P = True and Q = True → Result = ✔ True
If P = True and Q = False → Result = ✖ False
If P = False and Q = True → Result = ✖ False
If P = False and Q = False → Result = ✖ False
## Even if one condition is false, the entire conjunction becomes false.

## Example
Let:
P: x > 2  
Q: x < 10

Conjunction:
x > 2 AND x < 10
If x = 5
5 > 2 ✔
5 < 10 ✔
→ ✔ Satisfies
If x = 12
12 > 2 ✔
12 < 10 ✖
→ ✖ Does NOT satisfy
## Final Summary
Constraint → A rule that restricts possible solutions.
Conjunction (AND) → True only when both conditions are true.
If one condition fails → The entire statement becomes false.
